"""College-Level and Simplified wordings for Module I quiz questions, keyed by the existing question id.
Each entry: v(id, (college stem, correct, wrong, wrong, wrong), (simple stem, correct, wrong, wrong, wrong)).
The Original question bank in index.html is not edited. Collections 01 and 02."""
V = {}
def v(qid, college, simple):
    V[qid] = (college, simple)

# Collection 01 · Introduction to Cognitive Psychology
v(0, ('What did Donders (1868) use reaction time to estimate?', 'How long a mental operation, such as a decision, takes.', 'How long a stimulus stays in sensory memory.', 'How many items a person can hold in mind.', 'How quickly a learned list is forgotten.'),
     ('What did Donders (1868) measure to study the mind?', 'Reaction time.', 'Brain activity.', 'Reports of conscious experience.', 'The number of words remembered.'))
v(1, ('In the choice reaction time task, a participant presses one button for a left light and another for a right light. What does this add to the simple task?', 'A decision about which response to make.', 'A longer wait before the light appears.', 'A second button press on every trial.', 'A report of what the experience was like.'),
     ('What does the choice reaction time task add to the simple task?', 'A decision about which button to press.', 'A louder stimulus.', 'A memory test.', 'A longer wait before the light.'))
v(2, ('Choice reaction time is 0.10 seconds longer than simple reaction time. By Donders’ subtraction, how long does the decision take?', 'About 0.10 seconds.', 'The whole choice reaction time.', 'The whole simple reaction time.', 'About 1 second.'),
     ('How did Donders find the time a decision takes?', 'Choice reaction time minus simple reaction time.', 'Simple reaction time minus choice reaction time.', 'Choice reaction time plus simple reaction time.', 'Choice reaction time divided by two.'))
v(3, ('Why did analytic introspection fail to settle scientific disagreements?', 'The reports were difficult to quantify and replicate.', 'The reports could only be collected from children.', 'The method measured reaction time and nothing else.', 'The method removed conscious experience from the laboratory.'),
     ('What was the problem with introspection?', 'It was hard to measure and to repeat.', 'It took too long to learn.', 'It only worked for memory.', 'It needed expensive equipment.'))
v(4, ('What was Ebbinghaus (1885/1913) studying when he learned and relearned lists of nonsense syllables?', 'How quickly learned information is lost over time.', 'How long a decision takes.', 'How conscious experience is built from sensations.', 'How fear is conditioned.'),
     ('What did Ebbinghaus study?', 'Forgetting over time.', 'Reaction time.', 'Conditioned fear.', 'Language learning.'))
v(5, ('A list is relearned faster after a delay than it was first learned. Which measure from Ebbinghaus captures this?', 'Savings.', 'Reaction time.', 'Reinforcement.', 'Introspection.'),
     ('What is savings?', 'The original learning time minus the relearning time.', 'The relearning time minus the original learning time.', 'The number of syllables in a list.', 'The delay between learning and relearning.'))
v(6, ('What did Watson’s behaviorism say psychology should study?', 'Directly observable behavior.', 'Conscious experience reported by trained observers.', 'Internal mental processes inferred from behavior.', 'The functions of one’s own mind.'),
     ('What does behaviorism study?', 'Observable behavior.', 'The mind.', 'Internal mental processes.', 'Conscious experience.'))
v(7, ('In Watson and Rayner (1920), a rat was paired with a loud noise until the rat alone caused fear. Which kind of learning is this?', 'Classical conditioning.', 'Operant conditioning.', 'Relearning with savings.', 'Learning a cognitive map.'),
     ('What kind of conditioning was the Little Albert experiment?', 'Classical conditioning.', 'Operant conditioning.', 'Neither kind.', 'Both kinds equally.'))
v(8, ('In operant conditioning, what is a consequence called when it makes a behavior more likely?', 'Reinforcement.', 'Punishment.', 'A neutral stimulus.', 'Savings.'),
     ('What does reinforcement do to a behavior?', 'It makes the behavior more likely.', 'It makes the behavior less likely.', 'It has no effect on the behavior.', 'It replaces the behavior with a new one.'))
v(9, ('Tolman’s rats had learned to turn right to reach food. In Tolman (1938), started from a different arm, they turned left and reached the same food. What does this support?', 'The rats had formed a cognitive map of the maze.', 'The rats had learned only a right turn.', 'The rats had not learned where the food was.', 'The rats were responding to a new reward.'),
     ('What did Tolman say his rats had formed?', 'A cognitive map.', 'A habit of turning right.', 'A fear response.', 'A reaction time.'))
v(10, ('Which observation did Chomsky (1959) use against the claim that language is learned by imitation and reinforcement?', 'Children produce sentences they have never heard and make errors that were never rewarded.', 'Children hear a great deal of speech from adults.', 'Children are rewarded for correct speech.', 'Children imitate the words they hear most often.'),
      ('What did Chomsky point out about children’s language?', 'Children say sentences they have never heard.', 'Children only repeat what they hear.', 'Children learn language by reward alone.', 'Children cannot make errors.'))
v(11, ('What changed in psychology with the cognitive revolution of the 1950s?', 'The emphasis moved from stimulus and response relationships to the operations of the mind.', 'The emphasis moved from experiments to introspection.', 'Behavior was no longer measured.', 'The mind was removed as a topic of study.'),
      ('What did the cognitive revolution bring back?', 'The study of the mind.', 'The study of introspection only.', 'The study of conditioning only.', 'The study of reflexes only.'))
v(12, ('How did the Logic Theorist of Newell and Simon make a theory of reasoning testable?', 'It modeled problem solving as operations a machine could carry out.', 'It asked people to report their thoughts while reasoning.', 'It recorded reaction times for simple decisions.', 'It measured activity in the brain during reasoning.'),
      ('What did the Logic Theorist do?', 'It proved mathematical theorems.', 'It translated languages.', 'It measured reaction time.', 'It recognized faces.'))
v(13, ('A model shows the parts of the brain and where each part is. Which kind of model is it?', 'A structural model.', 'A process model.', 'A statistical model.', 'A savings curve.'),
      ('What does a structural model show?', 'The parts of a system and how they are organized.', 'The operations a system carries out over time.', 'The exact appearance of every detail.', 'The reaction time of a response.'))
v(14, ('A diagram has boxes for sensory memory, a filter and a detector, with arrows between them. Which kind of model is it?', 'A process model.', 'A structural model.', 'An exact copy of the brain.', 'A savings curve.'),
      ('In a process model, what do the arrows between the boxes show?', 'The flow of information.', 'The parts of the brain.', 'The order of the participants.', 'The size of each stage.'))
v(155, ('Which statements about scientific models are true? I. They simplify a system. II. They can generate predictions. III. They must copy every detail.', 'I and II only.', 'I and III only.', 'II and III only.', 'I, II and III.'),
       ('Is a model an exact copy of the real thing?', 'No. A model simplifies the system.', 'Yes. A model must copy every detail.', 'Yes, but only structural models.', 'Yes, but only process models.'))
v(159, ('A rat’s representation of a maze is different from the process of navigating. What is the representation?', 'Information about the layout that navigation can use.', 'The act of running through the maze.', 'The physical maze itself.', 'The reward at the end of the maze.'),
       ('What is a representation?', 'Information about something.', 'An operation on information.', 'A reaction time.', 'A reward.'))
v(160, ('Which of these is the scientific method that cognitive psychology uses?', 'Testing a proposed mental operation with measurements that can be repeated.', 'Accepting a claim because it agrees with intuition.', 'Relying on one trained observer’s report of experience.', 'Studying behavior without inferring anything about the mind.'),
       ('What kind of measurement does the scientific study of the mind need?', 'Quantifiable and repeatable measurement.', 'Measurement by one person only.', 'Measurement that cannot be checked.', 'No measurement.'))
v(161, ('When would Donders’ subtraction give a misleading estimate of decision time?', 'When the choice task also changes the time taken by the other steps.', 'When both tasks end with a button press.', 'When reaction times are recorded as numbers.', 'When the lights are easy to tell apart.'),
       ('What does Donders’ subtraction assume?', 'The steps the two tasks share take about the same time.', 'The two tasks have no steps in common.', 'The decision takes no time.', 'Reaction time cannot be measured.'))
v(162, ('A person cannot recall an old list but relearns it faster than a new list. What does this show?', 'Some of the earlier learning remains.', 'All of the earlier learning is gone.', 'The delay was too short to cause forgetting.', 'Relearning does not depend on earlier learning.'),
       ('Can savings show memory for a list that a person cannot recall?', 'Yes. Faster relearning shows that some learning remains.', 'No. If the list cannot be recalled, nothing remains.', 'No. Savings only measures reaction time.', 'Yes, but only after one day.'))
v(163, ('Which situation is classical conditioning and not operant conditioning?', 'A tone produces salivation after it has been paired with food.', 'A rewarded button press becomes more frequent.', 'A punished action becomes less frequent.', 'A child works harder after being praised.'),
       ('What is paired in classical conditioning?', 'Two stimuli.', 'An action and a reward.', 'An action and a punishment.', 'Two actions.'))
v(164, ('In operant conditioning, what is a consequence called when it makes the behavior it follows less likely?', 'Punishment.', 'Reinforcement.', 'A neutral stimulus.', 'Classical conditioning.'),
       ('What does punishment do to a behavior?', 'It makes the behavior less likely.', 'It makes the behavior more likely.', 'It has no effect.', 'It makes the behavior automatic.'))
v(165, ('How did the dichotic listening experiment of Cherry (1953) compare attended and unattended messages?', 'A different message goes to each ear, and the participant shadows one of them.', 'One message goes to both ears, and the participant repeats it.', 'Two messages are played one after the other in the same ear.', 'A message is played while the participant reads a different text silently.'),
       ('What is shadowing?', 'Repeating a message aloud as it arrives.', 'Listening to two messages at once.', 'Writing a message down afterward.', 'Ignoring a message.'))
v(166, ('What is the 1956 Dartmouth summer project known for?', 'Helping to establish artificial intelligence as a field.', 'Founding the first experimental psychology laboratory.', 'Publishing the behaviorist manifesto.', 'Introducing the filter model of attention.'),
       ('Which field is linked to the 1956 Dartmouth summer project?', 'Artificial intelligence.', 'Behaviorism.', 'Structuralism.', 'Psychophysics.'))
v(167, ('When is a simplified model still useful?', 'When it keeps the details that matter for the question.', 'When it includes every detail of the real system.', 'When it makes no predictions.', 'When it looks exactly like the real system.'),
       ('What does a model keep?', 'The details relevant to the question.', 'Every detail of the real thing.', 'No details at all.', 'Only the appearance.'))

# Collection 02 · Perception · Part 1
v(15, ('What is the inverse projection problem?', 'Determining which object produced a particular image on the retina.', 'Determining which wavelength of light reached the eye.', 'Recognizing an object from a new viewing angle.', 'Grouping the parts of a scene into objects.'),
      ('Can one retinal image come from more than one object?', 'Yes. Many different objects can produce the same image.', 'No. Each image comes from exactly one object.', 'Only if the objects are the same size.', 'Only if the objects are at the same distance.'))
v(16, ('A wheelbarrow is partly hidden behind a wall and is still seen as one wheelbarrow. How does the visual system treat the visible pieces?', 'As parts of one partly hidden object.', 'As several separate new shapes.', 'As the whole object with nothing hidden.', 'As a flat pattern with no depth.'),
      ('What is perceptual completion?', 'Extending the contours of an object behind what hides it.', 'Seeing movement between two flashes.', 'Recognizing an object from a new angle.', 'Grouping similar things together.'))
v(17, ('A chair is recognized as the same chair from many different angles. What is this ability called?', 'Viewpoint invariance.', 'Perceptual completion.', 'The oblique effect.', 'Apparent movement.'),
      ('What is viewpoint invariance?', 'Recognizing an object as the same object from different angles.', 'Recognizing an object that is partly hidden.', 'Seeing verticals more easily than obliques.', 'Seeing shading as depth.'))
v(18, ('Which processing starts when energy from the environment stimulates the receptors?', 'Bottom-up processing.', 'Top-down processing.', 'Perceptual completion.', 'Unconscious inference.'),
      ('Where does bottom-up processing start?', 'At the receptors.', 'In memory.', 'In expectations.', 'In attention.'))
v(19, ('You expect to see a friend, and a blurry figure looks like that friend. Which processing is this?', 'Top-down processing.', 'Bottom-up processing.', 'Viewpoint invariance.', 'Perceptual completion.'),
      ('What is another name for top-down processing?', 'Knowledge-driven processing.', 'Data-driven processing.', 'Receptor processing.', 'Direct perception.'))
v(20, ('Which theory says the light available to a moving observer is enough to specify the layout of the world?', 'Direct perception.', 'Constructive perception.', 'Unconscious inference.', 'Recognition by components.'),
      ('Who is associated with direct perception?', 'Gibson.', 'Helmholtz.', 'Biederman.', 'Wundt.'))
v(21, ('In Biederman’s recognition-by-components theory (1987), what are objects recognized from?', 'Geons and how they are arranged.', 'Whole stored images of each object.', 'The scene the object appears in.', 'The direction of light on the object.'),
      ('What are geons?', 'Basic three-dimensional building blocks of objects.', 'Neurons that respond to faces.', 'Rules for grouping lines.', 'Types of eye movements.'))
v(22, ('In the demonstration by Torralba and Oliva, the same blob is seen as a shoe in one scene and a pedestrian in another. What identifies it?', 'The context of the scene.', 'The shape of the blob.', 'The color of the blob.', 'The brightness of the blob.'),
      ('In Torralba and Oliva’s demonstration, were the blobs different?', 'No. The blobs were identical.', 'Yes. Each blob had a different shape.', 'Yes. Each blob had a different color.', 'Yes. Each blob was a different size.'))
v(23, ('Why can speech segmentation not be done by listening for silences between words?', 'Speech has almost no physical gaps at word boundaries.', 'Every word is followed by a long pause.', 'Listeners cannot hear pauses.', 'Word boundaries exist only in writing.'),
      ('What is speech segmentation?', 'Telling where one word ends and the next begins.', 'Telling one voice from another.', 'Filling in a missing sound.', 'Recognizing a letter in a word.'))
v(24, ('Pain changes with expectation when the stimulation stays the same. Which account does this go against?', 'The direct pathway model, which is purely bottom-up.', 'An account in which attention affects pain.', 'An account in which expectation affects pain.', 'An account that combines bottom-up and top-down influences.'),
      ('Does expectation change how much pain is felt?', 'Yes. Expectation, attention and distraction all change pain.', 'No. Pain follows tissue damage only.', 'Only attention changes pain.', 'Only distraction changes pain.'))
v(25, ('What does the likelihood principle of Helmholtz (around 1860) say?', 'We perceive the world in the way that is most likely, given our past experience.', 'We perceive every possible interpretation equally.', 'We perceive the simplest interpretation, whatever our experience.', 'We perceive nothing until we reason about it consciously.'),
      ('Is Helmholtz’s unconscious inference top-down or bottom-up?', 'Top-down.', 'Bottom-up.', 'Neither.', 'It is not about perception.'))
v(26, ('An image could come from two overlapping rectangles or from a rectangle and a notched shape lined up exactly. Why do people see two rectangles?', 'The notched shape needs a precise coincidence, and coincidences are rare.', 'The notched shape cannot produce that image.', 'The image labels which shape is in front.', 'People have never seen notched shapes.'),
      ('Which do people see, two overlapping rectangles or a rectangle and a notched shape?', 'Two overlapping rectangles.', 'A rectangle and a notched shape.', 'Both equally often.', 'Neither.'))
v(27, ('In Bayesian inference, what is the prior probability?', 'What you believed before the stimulus arrived.', 'The updated interpretation after the stimulus.', 'How well an interpretation explains the stimulus.', 'The stimulus itself.'),
      ('What is a prior?', 'A belief held before the evidence arrives.', 'A belief formed after the evidence arrives.', 'The evidence itself.', 'A reaction time.'))
v(28, ('In Bayesian inference, what is the likelihood?', 'How well each interpretation would explain the stimulus that was received.', 'What was believed before the stimulus arrived.', 'The updated interpretation.', 'How willing a person is to respond.'),
      ('What does the posterior combine?', 'The prior and the likelihood.', 'Two priors.', 'Two likelihoods.', 'The stimulus and the response.'))
v(29, ('A shaded disc looks like a bump, and it looks like a dent when the image is turned upside down. What explains this?', 'The assumption that light comes from above.', 'A change in the object that was photographed.', 'A loss of bottom-up information.', 'The law of similarity.'),
      ('What does the visual system assume about light?', 'Light comes from above.', 'Light comes from below.', 'Light comes from the left.', 'Light has no direction.'))
v(30, ('Why does apparent movement go against the idea that perception is the adding up of sensations?', 'Movement is seen although nothing moves through the space between.', 'An object really moves between the two positions.', 'The two stimuli are shown at the same time.', 'The movement is added by the eyes moving.'),
      ('What is apparent movement?', 'Seeing movement when nothing actually moves.', 'Seeing an object move faster than it does.', 'Missing an object that moves.', 'Seeing a still object while the eyes move.'))
v(31, ('You follow one cable through a tangle by taking the smoothest path. Which Gestalt law is this?', 'Good continuation.', 'Similarity.', 'Prägnanz.', 'Common region.'),
      ('What does good continuation say?', 'Lines are seen as following the smoothest path.', 'Similar things are grouped together.', 'Patterns are seen as simply as possible.', 'Nearby things are grouped together.'))
v(32, ('The Olympic symbol is seen as five circles and not as nine irregular shapes. Which Gestalt law is this?', 'Prägnanz, or simplicity.', 'Similarity.', 'Good continuation.', 'Figure and ground.'),
      ('What does Prägnanz mean?', 'A pattern is seen in the simplest possible way.', 'Similar things are grouped together.', 'Lines follow the smoothest path.', 'Nearby things are grouped together.'))
v(33, ('Dots of the same color are seen as columns although their positions did not change. Which Gestalt law is this?', 'Similarity.', 'Good continuation.', 'Prägnanz.', 'Apparent movement.'),
      ('What does the law of similarity say?', 'Similar things appear grouped together.', 'Lines follow the smoothest path.', 'Patterns are seen as simply as possible.', 'Movement is seen between flashes.'))
v(34, ('Knowing that kitchens usually contain stoves and sinks is an example of what?', 'Semantic regularities.', 'Physical regularities.', 'The oblique effect.', 'The light-from-above assumption.'),
      ('What are semantic regularities about?', 'What usually happens and appears in different types of scenes.', 'The direction of light.', 'The orientation of edges.', 'The color of surfaces.'))
v(35, ('People detect vertical and horizontal lines better than slanted lines. What is this called?', 'The oblique effect.', 'The light-from-above assumption.', 'Good continuation.', 'Viewpoint invariance.'),
      ('Which orientations do people perceive most easily?', 'Verticals and horizontals.', 'Obliques.', 'All orientations equally.', 'Curves.'))
v(36, ('What is damage to the ventral pathway most likely to impair?', 'Identifying objects.', 'Reaching for objects.', 'Locating objects.', 'Moving the eyes.'),
      ('What is the ventral pathway also called?', 'The what pathway.', 'The where pathway.', 'The how pathway.', 'The action pathway.'))
v(37, ('Patient D.F. could not report the orientation of a slot and could post a card through it accurately. What does this show?', 'Judging what something is and acting on it are separable.', 'She had no vision at all.', 'She could not use orientation in any task.', 'Judging and acting use one process.'),
      ('Which pathway was damaged in patient D.F.?', 'The ventral pathway.', 'The dorsal pathway.', 'Both pathways.', 'Neither pathway.'))
v(38, ('When do mirror neurons fire?', 'When an action is performed and when the same action is observed.', 'Only when an action is performed.', 'Only when an action is observed.', 'Only when an object is named.'),
      ('Where are mirror neurons found?', 'In the premotor cortex.', 'In the retina.', 'In the fusiform face area.', 'In the optic nerve.'))
v(151, ('Ventral damage impairs recognition and spares reaching. Dorsal damage impairs reaching and spares recognition. What do the two results together form?', 'A double dissociation.', 'A single general impairment.', 'A response bias.', 'An illusion.'),
       ('What does the dorsal pathway support?', 'Locating objects and guiding action toward them.', 'Identifying objects.', 'Recognizing faces.', 'Understanding speech.'))
v(153, ('Which of these give top-down knowledge for recognition? I. Scene schemas. II. Expectations about likely objects. III. Prior experience.', 'I, II and III.', 'I only.', 'II and III only.', 'I and II only.'),
       ('What is a scene schema?', 'Knowledge of what a kind of scene usually contains.', 'A map of the retina.', 'A type of eye movement.', 'A rule for grouping lines.'))
